WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.856
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:08.160
JACOB BANK: All right,
let's get started.

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:08.964
Hi, everyone.

00:00:08.964 --> 00:00:09.880
My name is Jacob Bank.

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:12.450
I'm the product lead
for Gmail, and I'm

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:14.640
going to be presenting
along with Ian Leader--

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:16.020
who's here in the front row.

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:18.840
He's the product lead
for Google Calendar,

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:21.240
and we're going to be
talking about time.

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:24.060
We're going to do a little
hero/villain routine.

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:25.500
I'm going to be
the villain that's

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:27.980
going to utterly
depress all of us

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:29.730
by explaining a bunch
of reasons why we're

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:31.500
terrible at managing
our time and how

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:32.756
hopeless the situation is.

00:00:32.756 --> 00:00:34.380
And then Ian's going
to be the hero who

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:36.660
comes saves us and tells us
about all the features we're

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:38.743
building in Gmail Calendar
that are trying to take

00:00:38.743 --> 00:00:40.980
these behavioral flaws
and mistakes into account

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:44.730
in the way we design products to
help people spend their time as

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:46.200
well as they can.

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:50.230
So we'll start with an
obligatory groan-inducing

00:00:50.230 --> 00:00:50.730
cliche--

00:00:50.730 --> 00:00:51.810
"Time is precious."

00:00:51.810 --> 00:00:54.289
But as we all collectively
roll our eyes,

00:00:54.289 --> 00:00:55.830
it's worth spending
a moment to think

00:00:55.830 --> 00:00:59.160
about why time is actually
pretty unique as a resource

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:01.876
and why it's particularly
difficult to manage because

00:01:01.876 --> 00:01:03.000
of these unique properties.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:04.121
You can't really save it.

00:01:04.121 --> 00:01:06.120
You can't really make
more of it unless you eat,

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:08.010
like, a barrel of
kale every day.

00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:10.680
All you can do is spend
it as wisely as possible.

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:13.349
Once it's spent, it's gone.

00:01:13.349 --> 00:01:15.640
It's an exercise that I go
through about once a month--

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:17.460
that I'd recommend
all of you go through,

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:20.850
if not now in real time,
maybe after the session ends.

00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:24.750
Over lunch, think about the
three most important things you

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:26.670
want to accomplish this year.

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:30.060
Then, look back at your calendar
for the last week, two weeks,

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:32.370
and try to figure out how
much time you actually

00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:35.310
spent on those three
most important things.

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:37.200
If you're anything
like me, it will

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:39.409
be a terrifyingly
small amount of time

00:01:39.409 --> 00:01:41.700
that you've spent on the
three things that are actually

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:43.908
important to you-- whether
it's in your personal life

00:01:43.908 --> 00:01:45.200
or your professional life.

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:47.700
And more generally, you can do
a more general exercise where

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:50.760
you draw a pie chart of
where your time is going now

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:53.700
and a pie chart of where
you'd like your time to go,

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:55.530
and there will probably
be a scary mismatch

00:01:55.530 --> 00:01:56.910
between those two.

00:01:56.910 --> 00:01:59.611
And it's not just us, it's
actually been studied a bunch.

00:01:59.611 --> 00:02:01.110
McKinsey did a study
a few years ago

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:02.790
where they interviewed
a bunch of executives

00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:05.123
and asked them how satisfied
they were with the way they

00:02:05.123 --> 00:02:06.150
manage their time.

00:02:06.150 --> 00:02:09.960
Only 9% said that they were
very satisfied with the way

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:13.770
that they manage their
time, and only 52%

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:16.080
said that the way they
spent their time largely

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:19.330
matched their organization's
strategic priorities.

00:02:19.330 --> 00:02:21.620
So while we spend hours
and hours haggling about

00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:23.370
do we launch this
feature or that feature?

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:24.536
Or can we save a penny here?

00:02:24.536 --> 00:02:25.730
Can we save a nickel there?

00:02:25.730 --> 00:02:27.438
We're maybe missing
out on an opportunity

00:02:27.438 --> 00:02:30.120
to save half of the time that
we're currently not putting

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:31.800
towards extra
strategic priorities

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:35.299
and line it up better
with our goals.

00:02:35.299 --> 00:02:36.840
So, where does that
time actually go?

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:38.673
McKinsey did another
study a couple of years

00:02:38.673 --> 00:02:43.800
ago on how a typical knowledge
worker spends their day,

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:45.090
and here are the results.

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:47.160
For the average knowledge
worker, about 28%

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:50.130
of their time reading and
answering email alone.

00:02:50.130 --> 00:02:53.280
It's almost three hours in
a typical 40-hour work week.

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:57.430
19% of their time searching
and gathering information,

00:02:57.430 --> 00:03:01.170
14% collaborating
internally-- meetings--

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:04.440
and 39% on role-specific
tasks, a.k.a.

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:07.080
the real work that these
people were all hired to do.

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:11.541
Only 39% of their time
spent on real work.

00:03:11.541 --> 00:03:13.520
So what we're going
to do in this talk,

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:14.649
my portion of the talk--

00:03:14.649 --> 00:03:16.440
I'm going to go through
a bunch of mistakes

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:19.350
that people make in
dealing with their email

00:03:19.350 --> 00:03:21.180
and dealing with
their schedules,

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:24.060
and cite some research
from behavioral psychology

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:27.120
and behavioral economics
that helps to explain why

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:31.110
our brains are wired to be bad
at using these tools to make

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:33.342
decisions about how
we spend our time.

00:03:33.342 --> 00:03:35.390
I'll start with email.

00:03:35.390 --> 00:03:38.370
The first and probably
most debilitating

00:03:38.370 --> 00:03:41.490
flaw we face in
dealing with our email

00:03:41.490 --> 00:03:43.980
is that it's a constant
source of interruption.

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:49.500
A typical knowledge worker
gets about 120 emails a day,

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:51.801
but what does that cost
in terms of interruption?

00:03:51.801 --> 00:03:53.550
Like, maybe I just
check my phone quickly,

00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:55.619
put it back in my
pocket, no harm done.

00:03:55.619 --> 00:03:57.660
Turns out, there's actually
been a research group

00:03:57.660 --> 00:04:00.960
that spent years
studying specifically

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:03.370
email interruptions
in the workplace

00:04:03.370 --> 00:04:06.630
and what the cost of
those interruptions is.

00:04:06.630 --> 00:04:10.260
And what they found is that when
you actually open your email

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:14.640
client from doing a creative
task like writing a paper

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:17.610
or preparing a presentation,
the average cost

00:04:17.610 --> 00:04:19.689
of that interruption
is 25 minutes.

00:04:19.689 --> 00:04:22.230
It's a pretty striking number,
and it's going to sound crazy.

00:04:22.230 --> 00:04:23.813
But if you actually
think through some

00:04:23.813 --> 00:04:26.070
of these scenarios, it'll
probably start to resonate.

00:04:26.070 --> 00:04:27.460
So, you get a notification
on your phone,

00:04:27.460 --> 00:04:29.001
you're like, oh
crap, that's my boss.

00:04:29.001 --> 00:04:29.970
I got to reply to that.

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:31.290
You open up your
email client, you

00:04:31.290 --> 00:04:32.520
realize that they're
asking for some number

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:34.470
that you don't have off
the top of your head.

00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:37.110
You go to some dashboard,
you find the number,

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:39.360
you compose a
well-crafted email,

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:40.950
you fire it off to your boss.

00:04:40.950 --> 00:04:42.450
By that time, another
email has come

00:04:42.450 --> 00:04:44.550
in that you need to reply to--

00:04:44.550 --> 00:04:46.110
repeat the same routine again.

00:04:46.110 --> 00:04:48.690
Then, someone sent you, like,
a video of a dog skateboarding

00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:49.290
on a Christmas tree.

00:04:49.290 --> 00:04:51.870
You've got to go watch that,
and then in a moment of panic,

00:04:51.870 --> 00:04:53.190
you realize that you
actually need to go back

00:04:53.190 --> 00:04:55.314
to writing your paper,
preparing your presentation,

00:04:55.314 --> 00:04:57.960
wherever the important creative
task is that you were doing.

00:04:57.960 --> 00:05:00.360
By that point, you've forgotten
what slide you were working on

00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:01.560
or what paragraph
you were writing

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:03.030
and what your train
of thought was,

00:05:03.030 --> 00:05:05.550
and you have to re-establish
all of that mental state

00:05:05.550 --> 00:05:07.620
before you can continue
to be creative.

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:10.440
And you get a number that's
kind of, like, 25 minutes.

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:13.470
So these interruptions are
extremely costly because we

00:05:13.470 --> 00:05:16.650
get distracted very easily.

00:05:16.650 --> 00:05:18.330
Second flaw, procrastination.

00:05:18.330 --> 00:05:21.660
This is one we've all
heard, experienced.

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:23.280
It's talked about a lot.

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:25.980
Usually, people's mental
model of procrastination

00:05:25.980 --> 00:05:28.950
is like a college student--
stays up all night

00:05:28.950 --> 00:05:30.990
eating pizza and
playing video games

00:05:30.990 --> 00:05:32.790
instead of working
on their term paper,

00:05:32.790 --> 00:05:34.800
but there are actually
much more subtle

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:38.970
and devious forms of
procrastination that plague us

00:05:38.970 --> 00:05:40.260
throughout the workday.

00:05:40.260 --> 00:05:43.830
And that's typically when
we do easy things that

00:05:43.830 --> 00:05:47.040
make us feel productive
and give us a quick win

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:50.040
but aren't actually contributing
to our long term productivity

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:51.290
or strategic goals.

00:05:51.290 --> 00:05:53.430
And a lot of this
happens in email.

00:05:53.430 --> 00:05:55.710
It's a lot easier and
feels a lot more productive

00:05:55.710 --> 00:05:58.079
to read a mailing list,
reply to an email,

00:05:58.079 --> 00:06:00.120
or chime in on a thread
that totally doesn't need

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:04.410
our opinion, rather than doing
the hard work of producing

00:06:04.410 --> 00:06:07.470
a document, or a presentation,
or the kind of creative work

00:06:07.470 --> 00:06:10.320
that taxes our
creative capacity.

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:12.557
We just can't
resist an easy win.

00:06:12.557 --> 00:06:14.640
And as a result, we don't
spend nearly enough time

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:17.586
on the hard but important stuff.

00:06:17.586 --> 00:06:20.700
Third, anyone heard of
Eisenhower's matrix?

00:06:20.700 --> 00:06:22.350
The Eisenhower matrix?

00:06:22.350 --> 00:06:24.450
It's like, you make
a two-by-two grid,

00:06:24.450 --> 00:06:27.300
and you put urgency on
one axis and importance

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:28.860
on the other axis.

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:32.280
And clearly, stuff that is
both urgent and important,

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:33.900
you should do all
of that stuff first.

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:36.570
Clearly, stuff that's
neither important or urgent,

00:06:36.570 --> 00:06:37.687
you should never do.

00:06:37.687 --> 00:06:40.020
And then the buckets where
people make a lot of mistakes

00:06:40.020 --> 00:06:42.810
are, they do things
that are urgent but not

00:06:42.810 --> 00:06:47.820
important over things that
are important but not urgent.

00:06:47.820 --> 00:06:50.217
And in the case of email,
similar to the procrastination

00:06:50.217 --> 00:06:52.800
example, a lot of emails-- we've
probably all seen emails that

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:53.940
have a subject, like, urgent--

00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:54.510
please read.

00:06:54.510 --> 00:06:56.694
Urgent-- action required.

00:06:56.694 --> 00:06:58.860
Depends how we define
urgency, but how many of those

00:06:58.860 --> 00:06:59.693
are actually urgent?

00:06:59.693 --> 00:07:01.290
Like, if you don't
reply this second,

00:07:01.290 --> 00:07:02.670
the world is going to explode.

00:07:02.670 --> 00:07:05.435
Probably not many of them.

00:07:05.435 --> 00:07:07.830
And so that same research
group that I mentioned

00:07:07.830 --> 00:07:09.810
that's done a lot of work
on email interruption

00:07:09.810 --> 00:07:13.860
has also studied email
batching behavior,

00:07:13.860 --> 00:07:17.730
and it turns out that if you do
fewer sessions in email a day--

00:07:17.730 --> 00:07:19.410
like, three long
sessions where you

00:07:19.410 --> 00:07:22.684
deal with all of your email
instead of 50 short sessions

00:07:22.684 --> 00:07:24.600
where you deal with only
one email at a time--

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:27.630
you're both more productive
and less stressed.

00:07:27.630 --> 00:07:29.520
But again, it's very
hard for us to resist

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:31.410
that, urgent-- please read.

00:07:31.410 --> 00:07:36.690
Urgent-- please reply, even
when it's not actually urgent,

00:07:36.690 --> 00:07:39.890
and we should be doing our
important work instead.

00:07:39.890 --> 00:07:42.520
Fourth, our inability to say no.

00:07:42.520 --> 00:07:44.270
How many of us have
ever received an email

00:07:44.270 --> 00:07:46.270
where someone else is
asking us to do something?

00:07:46.270 --> 00:07:47.210
Probably all of us.

00:07:47.210 --> 00:07:50.600
Probably, you all have like
10 in your inbox right now.

00:07:50.600 --> 00:07:55.760
Saying no is really hard because
social conventions dictate

00:07:55.760 --> 00:07:58.940
that we need to come up with a
really, really good excuse that

00:07:58.940 --> 00:08:01.970
makes us and the
requester feel comfortable

00:08:01.970 --> 00:08:05.360
about the rejection
of their request.

00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:07.760
And the cognitive load
of making these excuses

00:08:07.760 --> 00:08:09.920
is so high that we
often think, oh, I

00:08:09.920 --> 00:08:14.930
might as well just do the thing
rather than making the excuse.

00:08:14.930 --> 00:08:17.000
But rarely is that
actually the smart choice

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:19.610
in terms of time management
and prioritization.

00:08:19.610 --> 00:08:22.190
And because of this
social convention

00:08:22.190 --> 00:08:25.154
that makes it very
hard for us to say no,

00:08:25.154 --> 00:08:27.320
the result is that people
set our priorities for us.

00:08:27.320 --> 00:08:29.153
Like, a lot of people
have said things like,

00:08:29.153 --> 00:08:31.940
an email is like a to-do list
that other people write to.

00:08:31.940 --> 00:08:34.429
And it's true, largely, because
of the social convention

00:08:34.429 --> 00:08:38.419
that we feel obligated to
not only act, but act quickly

00:08:38.419 --> 00:08:41.350
on what people ask us to do.

00:08:41.350 --> 00:08:42.960
OK, we're halfway through--

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:45.502
halfway through.

00:08:45.502 --> 00:08:46.710
Number five, Parkinson's law.

00:08:46.710 --> 00:08:50.730
It's not actually a law,
but it's the principle

00:08:50.730 --> 00:08:53.250
that I think we've
all experienced--

00:08:53.250 --> 00:08:58.040
that work expands to fill
the time allotted to it.

00:08:58.040 --> 00:09:00.350
And this is particularly
true of meetings.

00:09:00.350 --> 00:09:03.450
So, most calendaring systems
default the meeting length

00:09:03.450 --> 00:09:04.302
to one hour.

00:09:04.302 --> 00:09:06.510
How many of you have ever
been in a meeting scheduled

00:09:06.510 --> 00:09:09.090
for an hour that
really only needed

00:09:09.090 --> 00:09:10.320
to happen for 20 minutes?

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:11.730
But everyone, you
know, stretches

00:09:11.730 --> 00:09:14.100
out the time for the full
hour anyway because you

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:16.433
got the room booked, you got
all the people in the room,

00:09:16.433 --> 00:09:18.785
might as well use it.

00:09:18.785 --> 00:09:21.160
But it turns out, this is
actually a huge waste of time--

00:09:21.160 --> 00:09:24.610
if you add up, cumulatively,
all these hour-long meetings

00:09:24.610 --> 00:09:26.680
that really should have
been half-hour meetings.

00:09:26.680 --> 00:09:29.138
And it's very hard to have the
discipline to stop a meeting

00:09:29.138 --> 00:09:32.290
when it's done and not when
the next person kicks you out

00:09:32.290 --> 00:09:34.540
of the meeting room.

00:09:34.540 --> 00:09:36.410
Number six, the
planning fallacy.

00:09:36.410 --> 00:09:39.850
This is an effect that was first
discovered or first written

00:09:39.850 --> 00:09:43.220
about in 1979 by
Tversky and Kahneman--

00:09:43.220 --> 00:09:45.910
the famous behavioral
economists--

00:09:45.910 --> 00:09:49.540
and it refers to our systematic
underestimation of how long

00:09:49.540 --> 00:09:50.710
things take.

00:09:50.710 --> 00:09:53.170
Turns out, we apply
an optimism bias

00:09:53.170 --> 00:09:55.030
whenever we're
projecting how long it's

00:09:55.030 --> 00:09:57.670
going to take for a
project to complete

00:09:57.670 --> 00:10:00.460
or for us to do even a
simple individual task.

00:10:00.460 --> 00:10:02.260
And the reason for
that is when we're

00:10:02.260 --> 00:10:05.950
making a projection on how long
a project is going to take,

00:10:05.950 --> 00:10:09.430
we have this optimistic
lens that everything

00:10:09.430 --> 00:10:11.800
is going to go smoothly
the entire time,

00:10:11.800 --> 00:10:14.500
even though no project in
the history of humanity

00:10:14.500 --> 00:10:16.550
has ever gone smoothly
the entire time.

00:10:16.550 --> 00:10:19.133
It's just a different thing that
goes wrong each time, so it's

00:10:19.133 --> 00:10:20.950
very hard to reason about.

00:10:20.950 --> 00:10:25.545
And how off we are depends a
lot based on the circumstance,

00:10:25.545 --> 00:10:26.920
and there's a
bunch of tricks you

00:10:26.920 --> 00:10:29.592
can use to try to be more
accurate at estimating.

00:10:29.592 --> 00:10:31.300
But a good rule of
thumb is that projects

00:10:31.300 --> 00:10:33.800
will take about double
what we think they will.

00:10:33.800 --> 00:10:35.880
It's a good rule of
thumb that's been proven

00:10:35.880 --> 00:10:39.490
across a variety of cases,
and the really particularly

00:10:39.490 --> 00:10:41.800
depressing thing about
the planning fallacy

00:10:41.800 --> 00:10:43.892
is that even if you tell
someone right before

00:10:43.892 --> 00:10:45.850
they're about to make an
estimation of how long

00:10:45.850 --> 00:10:47.850
something is going to take--

00:10:47.850 --> 00:10:50.320
that we are subject to
this behavioral flaw--

00:10:50.320 --> 00:10:51.820
they're still going
to underestimate

00:10:51.820 --> 00:10:52.870
how long it's going to take.

00:10:52.870 --> 00:10:54.495
Because again, they're
not going to see

00:10:54.495 --> 00:10:56.620
all of the little things
that will go wrong

00:10:56.620 --> 00:10:58.030
that are abstracted
away when you

00:10:58.030 --> 00:11:00.220
have an optimistic and
rosy picture of what's

00:11:00.220 --> 00:11:02.290
going to happen.

00:11:02.290 --> 00:11:06.160
Related to the underestimation
of how long things will take

00:11:06.160 --> 00:11:08.680
is the overestimation
of how available

00:11:08.680 --> 00:11:10.100
we'll be in the future.

00:11:10.100 --> 00:11:12.670
So if I ask any one of you,
can you spend four hours

00:11:12.670 --> 00:11:14.250
with me this afternoon meeting?

00:11:14.250 --> 00:11:15.330
You'll say, no
way, that's crazy.

00:11:15.330 --> 00:11:15.940
I'm at a conference.

00:11:15.940 --> 00:11:16.680
I'm super busy.

00:11:16.680 --> 00:11:18.138
I've got a bunch
of meetings to do,

00:11:18.138 --> 00:11:20.024
I can't possibly spend
four hours with you.

00:11:20.024 --> 00:11:21.940
If I ask you, can you
spend four hours with me

00:11:21.940 --> 00:11:23.090
over the week of July 20?

00:11:23.090 --> 00:11:25.090
You'll look at your
calender and say, yeah, sure

00:11:25.090 --> 00:11:27.570
I've got four weeks on
the week of July 20.

00:11:27.570 --> 00:11:29.320
Now in reality, you're
going to be just as

00:11:29.320 --> 00:11:31.300
busy the week of July
20 as you are now.

00:11:31.300 --> 00:11:33.820
You just don't know what the
things are yet that you're

00:11:33.820 --> 00:11:36.430
going to be busy with.

00:11:36.430 --> 00:11:39.460
And the further we are in
the future, the less and less

00:11:39.460 --> 00:11:42.070
concrete the other obligations
that are vying for our time

00:11:42.070 --> 00:11:44.230
are, so the more
we underestimate

00:11:44.230 --> 00:11:45.130
how busy we'll be.

00:11:45.130 --> 00:11:46.120
And that's how you
end up doing things,

00:11:46.120 --> 00:11:47.950
like, two years
from now, you commit

00:11:47.950 --> 00:11:49.300
to a two-week-long workshop.

00:11:49.300 --> 00:11:50.530
And then by the
time you get there,

00:11:50.530 --> 00:11:52.060
you can't imagine
how you ever would

00:11:52.060 --> 00:11:53.560
have committed to that
because you clearly don't

00:11:53.560 --> 00:11:54.643
have enough time to do it.

00:11:58.120 --> 00:12:00.520
Last one, productive time.

00:12:00.520 --> 00:12:04.370
So, our cognitive capacity
varies throughout the day,

00:12:04.370 --> 00:12:06.250
and if you're like
most people, you're

00:12:06.250 --> 00:12:10.030
at your most productive in the
morning between roughly 8:00 AM

00:12:10.030 --> 00:12:10.817
and 11:00 AM.

00:12:10.817 --> 00:12:13.150
Usually, like, after you've
had your first cup of coffee

00:12:13.150 --> 00:12:16.444
before you get super
hungry for lunch.

00:12:16.444 --> 00:12:18.610
And if you're like most
people-- if you're like me--

00:12:18.610 --> 00:12:20.818
that's when you're, kind
of, rolling into the office,

00:12:20.818 --> 00:12:23.980
you're checking email,
catching up on the news,

00:12:23.980 --> 00:12:27.310
easing your way into the day.

00:12:27.310 --> 00:12:28.809
And then after lunch--

00:12:28.809 --> 00:12:30.850
when we're all at our
least productive, typically

00:12:30.850 --> 00:12:32.392
the post-lunch dip,
1:00 to 2:00 PM--

00:12:32.392 --> 00:12:34.725
you realize you actually need
to get your real work done

00:12:34.725 --> 00:12:36.580
and try to buckle
down, but simply don't

00:12:36.580 --> 00:12:38.530
have the energy to do so.

00:12:38.530 --> 00:12:41.740
And that's because we do a
very poor job of recognizing

00:12:41.740 --> 00:12:45.070
how our cognitive capacity
changes throughout the day,

00:12:45.070 --> 00:12:48.970
and how to align the cognitive
capacity of our mental state

00:12:48.970 --> 00:12:51.670
with the cognitive capacity
required by the tasks

00:12:51.670 --> 00:12:53.490
that we need to do.

00:12:53.490 --> 00:12:55.920
So we misuse our
most precious hours

00:12:55.920 --> 00:12:58.776
pretty much every morning.

00:12:58.776 --> 00:13:00.650
So we add all of this
up, we get interrupted,

00:13:00.650 --> 00:13:02.900
we procrastinate, we do
things that are urgent but not

00:13:02.900 --> 00:13:05.660
important, we can't say no,
we waste a bunch of time

00:13:05.660 --> 00:13:08.090
in meetings that should've
ended more quickly,

00:13:08.090 --> 00:13:10.340
we underestimate how long
everything is going to take,

00:13:10.340 --> 00:13:12.440
we overestimate how
much time we have,

00:13:12.440 --> 00:13:14.330
we misuse our most
precious hours a day--

00:13:14.330 --> 00:13:17.300
it's not a shock that people
think that 50% of their time

00:13:17.300 --> 00:13:19.310
is not going to their
strategic priorities.

00:13:19.310 --> 00:13:21.920
And it's a bleak outlook--
it's kind of a downer.

00:13:21.920 --> 00:13:24.950
It feels a little bit hopeless.

00:13:24.950 --> 00:13:26.480
But now I'll turn
it over to Ian,

00:13:26.480 --> 00:13:28.430
and Ian's going to tell us
about a bunch of the features

00:13:28.430 --> 00:13:30.680
that we're building in Google
Calendar and into Gmail

00:13:30.680 --> 00:13:33.440
to take into account all of
these behavioral principles

00:13:33.440 --> 00:13:35.690
that make us naturally
bad at managing time.

00:13:35.690 --> 00:13:37.670
And hopefully, we
can make it better.

00:13:37.670 --> 00:13:38.878
IAN LEADER: Thank you, Jacob.

00:13:44.460 --> 00:13:47.160
OK, so let's talk solutions.

00:13:47.160 --> 00:13:48.960
The problems that Jacob
described probably

00:13:48.960 --> 00:13:50.530
aren't new to a lot of you.

00:13:50.530 --> 00:13:53.310
There's hundreds of books,
online articles, training

00:13:53.310 --> 00:13:56.730
courses about how to manage
your time, but most of us

00:13:56.730 --> 00:13:57.870
are still doing a poor job.

00:13:57.870 --> 00:14:01.660
So clearly, reading a book on
productivity is not enough.

00:14:01.660 --> 00:14:04.990
So, how do we solve this?

00:14:04.990 --> 00:14:07.230
The renowned behavioral
economist Dan Ariely

00:14:07.230 --> 00:14:10.110
often says that, like, one
of the biggest lessons we've

00:14:10.110 --> 00:14:13.440
had from behavioral economics
is that decisions we make

00:14:13.440 --> 00:14:16.320
are shaped by the
environment that we're in.

00:14:16.320 --> 00:14:18.750
A great example of this--

00:14:18.750 --> 00:14:21.330
from outside the workplace--
is contribution to retirement

00:14:21.330 --> 00:14:23.209
plans like 401(k)s.

00:14:23.209 --> 00:14:24.750
There's a large body
of research that

00:14:24.750 --> 00:14:27.390
shows that most people
will only save enough

00:14:27.390 --> 00:14:31.019
during their working
life if, by default,

00:14:31.019 --> 00:14:33.060
contributions to the plan
out of their pay packet

00:14:33.060 --> 00:14:34.810
happen automatically.

00:14:34.810 --> 00:14:38.460
So, we think that the best
way to help people improve

00:14:38.460 --> 00:14:39.930
their time management
behavior is

00:14:39.930 --> 00:14:43.080
to change the environment
they're operating in,

00:14:43.080 --> 00:14:45.054
and that raises the
question at work,

00:14:45.054 --> 00:14:46.470
which environment
should we change

00:14:46.470 --> 00:14:49.900
to get the biggest benefit?

00:14:49.900 --> 00:14:51.950
And we think Gmail
and Calendar are key,

00:14:51.950 --> 00:14:54.660
and that's not just
because we work on them.

00:14:54.660 --> 00:14:56.310
To repeat the
figures from McKinsey

00:14:56.310 --> 00:14:58.800
that Jacob mentioned earlier,
the average knowledge worker

00:14:58.800 --> 00:15:02.380
is spending almost three hours
a day in which environment?

00:15:02.380 --> 00:15:05.490
In their email client
and their calendar.

00:15:05.490 --> 00:15:08.820
So around 90% of the
large companies that

00:15:08.820 --> 00:15:12.360
use G Suite today,
employee calendars

00:15:12.360 --> 00:15:14.636
are shared with the
entire organization.

00:15:14.636 --> 00:15:17.010
That means, anyone can look
and see when you're available

00:15:17.010 --> 00:15:18.690
and schedule a meeting with you.

00:15:18.690 --> 00:15:21.300
This is a really powerful
tool for collaboration,

00:15:21.300 --> 00:15:23.490
but it can have the
unfortunate side effect

00:15:23.490 --> 00:15:28.050
that people start to
manage your time for you.

00:15:28.050 --> 00:15:31.080
And as Jacob said, once
that meeting invite arrives,

00:15:31.080 --> 00:15:34.190
we're not actually
very good at saying no.

00:15:34.190 --> 00:15:36.612
So first of all, I'm going
to look at some of the tools

00:15:36.612 --> 00:15:38.570
that we already have in
Gmail and Calendar that

00:15:38.570 --> 00:15:39.944
will help you,
and then I'm going

00:15:39.944 --> 00:15:42.950
to talk about the future of
things we haven't released yet

00:15:42.950 --> 00:15:46.220
that we're thinking about
as new ways to do this.

00:15:46.220 --> 00:15:48.679
OK, so Priority Inbox.

00:15:48.679 --> 00:15:51.220
It's been around for a while,
many of you may have seen this.

00:15:51.220 --> 00:15:53.095
It was one of the early
intelligence features

00:15:53.095 --> 00:15:57.740
that we put into
Gmail back in 2010.

00:15:57.740 --> 00:16:01.160
And so, Priority Inbox
uses a variety of signals

00:16:01.160 --> 00:16:03.680
to work out which emails
are likely important to you,

00:16:03.680 --> 00:16:05.900
and it shows these
at the top separately

00:16:05.900 --> 00:16:08.329
from all the other emails.

00:16:08.329 --> 00:16:09.370
So, what kind of signals?

00:16:09.370 --> 00:16:12.410
For example, we look at
who you email to the most

00:16:12.410 --> 00:16:15.010
because emails coming
to you from those people

00:16:15.010 --> 00:16:17.250
are likely important to you.

00:16:17.250 --> 00:16:20.720
And we also look
at which emails you

00:16:20.720 --> 00:16:24.530
read, which emails you open,
which emails you reply to,

00:16:24.530 --> 00:16:26.780
and we consider emails
similar to those

00:16:26.780 --> 00:16:29.060
more important than
the kind of emails

00:16:29.060 --> 00:16:30.830
that you typically skip over.

00:16:30.830 --> 00:16:34.610
And so what all of that
means is when you open Gmail,

00:16:34.610 --> 00:16:36.410
you're looking at
the important stuff

00:16:36.410 --> 00:16:40.040
with fewer distractions that
can lead to procrastination.

00:16:40.040 --> 00:16:42.050
And there's also
fewer interruptions

00:16:42.050 --> 00:16:44.157
because you can set
your phone to buzz only

00:16:44.157 --> 00:16:45.740
when it's one of
these priority emails

00:16:45.740 --> 00:16:49.296
that are arriving--
not the rest.

00:16:49.296 --> 00:16:51.170
And we have another tool
in the product today

00:16:51.170 --> 00:16:53.460
that's also around
elevating important emails,

00:16:53.460 --> 00:16:55.480
and that's Tabbed Inbox.

00:16:55.480 --> 00:16:57.260
And it's ideal if
you want more control

00:16:57.260 --> 00:16:59.270
than Priority Inbox
gives you about how

00:16:59.270 --> 00:17:01.470
your email is divided up.

00:17:01.470 --> 00:17:04.229
Particularly, if you
subscribe to a lot of forums,

00:17:04.229 --> 00:17:06.020
or if you get a lot of
updates from systems

00:17:06.020 --> 00:17:10.099
like workflow systems or
project management systems.

00:17:10.099 --> 00:17:13.250
And Tabbed Inbox automatically
categorizes the email,

00:17:13.250 --> 00:17:15.260
so there's forums
like Google Groups.

00:17:15.260 --> 00:17:17.060
There's updates, like,
an invoice coming in

00:17:17.060 --> 00:17:17.809
for your approval.

00:17:17.809 --> 00:17:20.990
And then there's the Primary
Inbox, which is usually

00:17:20.990 --> 00:17:22.630
stuff from real people.

00:17:22.630 --> 00:17:25.369
And like Priority Inbox,
this reduces distractions

00:17:25.369 --> 00:17:28.010
and makes it easier to batch
up those email sessions

00:17:28.010 --> 00:17:30.550
into fewer, larger sessions.

00:17:30.550 --> 00:17:32.830
It's changing time slightly.

00:17:32.830 --> 00:17:36.400
One of the reasons people allow
themselves to get distracted

00:17:36.400 --> 00:17:38.410
by non-urgent emails is
because they keep them

00:17:38.410 --> 00:17:40.840
around in their
inbox because they're

00:17:40.840 --> 00:17:44.810
scared that if they don't,
then they'll forget about them.

00:17:44.810 --> 00:17:47.260
And they'll drop the ball,
and they won't follow up.

00:17:47.260 --> 00:17:49.520
So, we introduced
the snooze feature,

00:17:49.520 --> 00:17:52.940
which will let you hide an
email until a future time--

00:17:52.940 --> 00:17:55.660
or until you're in
a future location.

00:17:55.660 --> 00:17:58.600
And that gets it out of the
way, but doesn't give you

00:17:58.600 --> 00:18:01.990
the cognitive load of having
to remember to get back to it.

00:18:01.990 --> 00:18:05.200
Instead, we'll bump it
to the top of your inbox

00:18:05.200 --> 00:18:09.430
when that time or place
arrives, so you can't miss it.

00:18:09.430 --> 00:18:12.520
And snoozing is available
in the inbox client,

00:18:12.520 --> 00:18:14.110
which alongside
Gmail, works well

00:18:14.110 --> 00:18:15.970
for a variety of
different workflows,

00:18:15.970 --> 00:18:19.450
including the zero
inbox approach.

00:18:19.450 --> 00:18:21.730
So let's take a look
at Calendar, now.

00:18:21.730 --> 00:18:24.040
Jacob talked about
the one-hour meetings

00:18:24.040 --> 00:18:27.010
that really need to be one
hour, so what if you just

00:18:27.010 --> 00:18:28.240
had shorter meetings?

00:18:28.240 --> 00:18:31.240
Well, when you start using
Google Calendar in G Suite

00:18:31.240 --> 00:18:34.420
today, the default length
for meetings is one hour.

00:18:34.420 --> 00:18:37.095
Maybe we should change
that, but you can set it

00:18:37.095 --> 00:18:37.970
to anything you like.

00:18:37.970 --> 00:18:40.280
You can set it 10
minutes, 15 minutes,

00:18:40.280 --> 00:18:41.740
30 minutes what have you.

00:18:41.740 --> 00:18:44.694
Now, I'm not saying that you
should make every meeting 10

00:18:44.694 --> 00:18:46.360
minutes long, although,
it would be nice

00:18:46.360 --> 00:18:48.280
if that was actually possible.

00:18:48.280 --> 00:18:49.990
But by using the
power of defaults,

00:18:49.990 --> 00:18:53.290
you can change the environment
so that your default of, say,

00:18:53.290 --> 00:18:55.720
15 minutes makes it
less work to schedule

00:18:55.720 --> 00:18:59.810
a short meeting than it does
to schedule a long meeting.

00:18:59.810 --> 00:19:02.350
So that's a pretty simple hack
to help reduce your meeting

00:19:02.350 --> 00:19:05.440
load, but we've also been
working on some more, sort of,

00:19:05.440 --> 00:19:07.360
complex problems and solutions.

00:19:07.360 --> 00:19:12.040
In particular, how can we help
people spend more time focusing

00:19:12.040 --> 00:19:16.690
on long-term productive tasks
despite this tendency to become

00:19:16.690 --> 00:19:18.850
distracted and procrastinate?

00:19:18.850 --> 00:19:21.942
So, let me quickly
talk about a study that

00:19:21.942 --> 00:19:24.400
was published in the "British
Journal of Health Psychology"

00:19:24.400 --> 00:19:25.236
a few years ago.

00:19:25.236 --> 00:19:26.860
And it was about,
how do you get people

00:19:26.860 --> 00:19:29.530
to exercise more frequently?

00:19:29.530 --> 00:19:33.010
So, there were three groups
of participants in this study,

00:19:33.010 --> 00:19:34.390
in the first group--

00:19:34.390 --> 00:19:37.270
in the control group, which is
people in their natural state,

00:19:37.270 --> 00:19:39.340
just let them do
what they want--

00:19:39.340 --> 00:19:43.270
around 38% of the
participants did

00:19:43.270 --> 00:19:44.950
exercise at least once a week.

00:19:44.950 --> 00:19:46.840
And so the question
is, how can we

00:19:46.840 --> 00:19:49.880
get more people to do exercise?

00:19:49.880 --> 00:19:52.430
So, the first thing they did
is they took the second group--

00:19:52.430 --> 00:19:54.100
they called the
motivation group--

00:19:54.100 --> 00:19:55.510
and they tried to motivate them.

00:19:55.510 --> 00:19:58.310
They gave them a brochure on
why exercise is good for you.

00:19:58.310 --> 00:20:00.310
They talked to them about
coronary heart disease

00:20:00.310 --> 00:20:01.810
and how terribly
unpleasant this is,

00:20:01.810 --> 00:20:04.240
and how exercise can
actually help overcome this.

00:20:04.240 --> 00:20:06.573
And so you'd think that you
would get a positive outcome

00:20:06.573 --> 00:20:07.810
from this, but guess what?

00:20:07.810 --> 00:20:09.040
You didn't.

00:20:09.040 --> 00:20:11.620
In fact, there was a
slight drop in the number

00:20:11.620 --> 00:20:14.620
of participants in that group
exercising at least once

00:20:14.620 --> 00:20:15.920
a week.

00:20:15.920 --> 00:20:18.040
However, there was
a third group--

00:20:18.040 --> 00:20:21.910
the intention group--
and in this group,

00:20:21.910 --> 00:20:25.550
the participants were asked
to commit a day and a time

00:20:25.550 --> 00:20:27.320
when you will do you
exercise next week,

00:20:27.320 --> 00:20:29.060
and the week after, and so on.

00:20:29.060 --> 00:20:32.400
And here, the
impact was massive.

00:20:32.400 --> 00:20:34.680
There we go.

00:20:34.680 --> 00:20:36.100
We actually saw that--

00:20:36.100 --> 00:20:39.379
in that study they saw
that 91% of participants

00:20:39.379 --> 00:20:40.920
were exercising at
least once a week,

00:20:40.920 --> 00:20:45.370
and that's a 160%
increase over the control.

00:20:45.370 --> 00:20:49.560
So, when we think about
this in a work context,

00:20:49.560 --> 00:20:50.505
what does it tell us?

00:20:50.505 --> 00:20:54.020
Like, being motivated is
not, on its own, enough.

00:20:54.020 --> 00:20:55.666
You're motivated, right?

00:20:55.666 --> 00:20:57.290
You all want to do
a great job at work.

00:20:57.290 --> 00:20:58.740
You all want to have an impact.

00:20:58.740 --> 00:21:01.200
None of you want to get fired.

00:21:01.200 --> 00:21:04.200
But that, on its own, is
not enough to overcome

00:21:04.200 --> 00:21:06.810
these cognitive failures
that get in the way of how

00:21:06.810 --> 00:21:09.230
we spend our time well.

00:21:09.230 --> 00:21:11.490
However, scheduling
can make a difference.

00:21:11.490 --> 00:21:13.130
The problem is,
scheduling everything

00:21:13.130 --> 00:21:14.880
that you're going to
do into your calendar

00:21:14.880 --> 00:21:16.400
is actually hard work.

00:21:16.400 --> 00:21:18.960
And so many people, even
when they know about this,

00:21:18.960 --> 00:21:21.750
they don't do that extra
work of scheduling.

00:21:21.750 --> 00:21:29.080
So what we decided to
do was build a feature

00:21:29.080 --> 00:21:32.200
into the Google Calendar
product called Goals.

00:21:32.200 --> 00:21:33.925
And this is about
taking the work out

00:21:33.925 --> 00:21:35.800
of scheduling time for
these long-term goals.

00:21:35.800 --> 00:21:38.710
And we launched it
almost a year ago.

00:21:38.710 --> 00:21:42.670
Basically, you tell Calendar
what you want to do,

00:21:42.670 --> 00:21:44.860
how long you want to do
it for, and how often

00:21:44.860 --> 00:21:46.090
you want to do it.

00:21:46.090 --> 00:21:48.040
And Calendar takes
care of setting up

00:21:48.040 --> 00:21:49.810
the sessions by
automatically blocking out

00:21:49.810 --> 00:21:52.790
time on your calendar.

00:21:52.790 --> 00:21:54.860
So, how do we find
good times for you

00:21:54.860 --> 00:21:57.980
to do this if all you're telling
us is how frequently you want

00:21:57.980 --> 00:22:01.400
to exercise, or how frequently
you want to spend focused time?

00:22:01.400 --> 00:22:04.040
So, we start by picking
times that, in general, make

00:22:04.040 --> 00:22:05.090
sense for everyone.

00:22:05.090 --> 00:22:08.270
For example, if it's around
focused productive time

00:22:08.270 --> 00:22:09.680
in the morning.

00:22:09.680 --> 00:22:11.900
We obviously look at what's
in your Calendar already,

00:22:11.900 --> 00:22:13.316
because we wouldn't
be helping you

00:22:13.316 --> 00:22:16.250
if we schedule time over
an existing meeting.

00:22:16.250 --> 00:22:18.380
And then, the system
learns over time.

00:22:18.380 --> 00:22:22.400
So for example, if we
scheduled every Tuesday

00:22:22.400 --> 00:22:24.290
at 6:00 PM for
you to go running,

00:22:24.290 --> 00:22:27.380
and you always check that as
done, we know it works for you,

00:22:27.380 --> 00:22:29.570
and we'll keep
scheduling it then.

00:22:29.570 --> 00:22:33.740
But on the other hand,
if we put productive time

00:22:33.740 --> 00:22:37.002
for coding into your calendar
every Wednesday morning,

00:22:37.002 --> 00:22:38.960
and you keep moving it
to Tuesday and Thursday,

00:22:38.960 --> 00:22:40.720
then we'll learn that
Wednesday isn't good

00:22:40.720 --> 00:22:43.850
and start scheduling
it on the other days.

00:22:43.850 --> 00:22:47.360
Now, we designed Goals to
work with a broad range

00:22:47.360 --> 00:22:49.700
of different types.

00:22:49.700 --> 00:22:52.370
Exercise is very, very
popular, but we also

00:22:52.370 --> 00:22:54.980
see quite a lot of people
using it for work-centric goals

00:22:54.980 --> 00:22:58.070
like having a daily block
for planning in the calendar

00:22:58.070 --> 00:23:02.630
or focused time like the coding
example you can see here.

00:23:02.630 --> 00:23:05.930
So, this sort of
takes the effort out

00:23:05.930 --> 00:23:10.040
of doing the scheduling, so you
don't have to do it manually.

00:23:10.040 --> 00:23:11.840
Does it still have
the desired effect

00:23:11.840 --> 00:23:14.150
of getting you to commit more?

00:23:14.150 --> 00:23:15.789
And it turns out
that, yes, it does--

00:23:15.789 --> 00:23:16.580
I'm pleased to say.

00:23:16.580 --> 00:23:18.020
So last summer
after we launched,

00:23:18.020 --> 00:23:22.090
we ran an eight-week
95-participant study

00:23:22.090 --> 00:23:24.680
with people using this
feature, and by the end of it,

00:23:24.680 --> 00:23:28.430
74% said that it was helping
them to meet their goals.

00:23:28.430 --> 00:23:31.250
And perhaps more
importantly, on average,

00:23:31.250 --> 00:23:34.340
participants were
spending 30% more time

00:23:34.340 --> 00:23:36.740
on their goals at
the end of the study

00:23:36.740 --> 00:23:38.690
than they were at the beginning.

00:23:38.690 --> 00:23:42.286
And we also saw a big increase
in the actual amount of time

00:23:42.286 --> 00:23:43.160
people were spending.

00:23:43.160 --> 00:23:45.730
So as you can see
from this chart,

00:23:45.730 --> 00:23:49.040
there was a 100% increase in
the number of participants

00:23:49.040 --> 00:23:52.970
who were spending four or
more hours a week on what they

00:23:52.970 --> 00:23:54.680
described as their
most important goal.

00:23:54.680 --> 00:23:57.710
So great, we think it works.

00:23:57.710 --> 00:24:00.715
OK so, let's move
on to the future.

00:24:00.715 --> 00:24:02.840
I've just taken you through
some of the things that

00:24:02.840 --> 00:24:04.370
are in the product today--

00:24:04.370 --> 00:24:06.320
you can use it right now--

00:24:06.320 --> 00:24:08.840
and I want to take you through
some sorts of explorations.

00:24:08.840 --> 00:24:10.757
How we're thinking
about improving

00:24:10.757 --> 00:24:11.840
the product in the future.

00:24:11.840 --> 00:24:13.689
Now, we're really excited
about these ideas.

00:24:13.689 --> 00:24:15.230
We're excited to
tell you about them.

00:24:15.230 --> 00:24:16.880
We'd love to get
feedback from you,

00:24:16.880 --> 00:24:18.739
but please remember
they're explorations.

00:24:18.739 --> 00:24:20.030
They're not committed features.

00:24:20.030 --> 00:24:22.520
So, they might not end up
in the product in the form

00:24:22.520 --> 00:24:23.660
that you are about to see.

00:24:23.660 --> 00:24:26.510
They might not end up
in the product at all.

00:24:26.510 --> 00:24:29.120
That caveat aside, let's
have a look at what

00:24:29.120 --> 00:24:31.320
we're thinking about for email.

00:24:31.320 --> 00:24:38.650
So, Priority Inbox
and Tabbed Inbox

00:24:38.650 --> 00:24:40.740
are good at getting
rid of the clutter,

00:24:40.740 --> 00:24:45.740
but for very busy
email inboxes, there's

00:24:45.740 --> 00:24:47.530
still a lot of emails in there.

00:24:47.530 --> 00:24:50.870
And it's hard for people to know
which email to look at first.

00:24:50.870 --> 00:24:54.560
So, the Highlights exploration,
which you can see here,

00:24:54.560 --> 00:24:58.370
builds on Priority Inbox by more
accurately elevating the most

00:24:58.370 --> 00:24:59.660
important and urgent emails.

00:24:59.660 --> 00:25:03.440
And it does this by using
NLP to actually look at what

00:25:03.440 --> 00:25:05.060
is the content of the email?

00:25:05.060 --> 00:25:07.430
And so, for example, it
could elevate an email

00:25:07.430 --> 00:25:10.009
where someone has
requested a response today.

00:25:10.009 --> 00:25:12.050
Particularly, if that's
also someone that we know

00:25:12.050 --> 00:25:13.790
is important to you.

00:25:13.790 --> 00:25:15.290
And in addition to
that, it actually

00:25:15.290 --> 00:25:18.600
shows a rationale, which you can
see in gray above each email,

00:25:18.600 --> 00:25:20.930
explaining why
we've elevated that.

00:25:20.930 --> 00:25:22.880
So you can more
easily make a decision

00:25:22.880 --> 00:25:27.500
about whether you want to
act on my email now or later.

00:25:27.500 --> 00:25:31.370
In working together with this,
we have a second exploration--

00:25:31.370 --> 00:25:35.250
the limited notifications
exploration.

00:25:35.250 --> 00:25:38.100
And this is about getting
fewer interruptions

00:25:38.100 --> 00:25:41.010
by only being notified
when those most

00:25:41.010 --> 00:25:43.860
important emails arrive.

00:25:43.860 --> 00:25:47.010
And it's not just about looking
at the most important emails,

00:25:47.010 --> 00:25:49.500
but it also takes your
context into account.

00:25:49.500 --> 00:25:51.180
What are you doing?

00:25:51.180 --> 00:25:52.830
So if you're in a
meeting, for example,

00:25:52.830 --> 00:25:55.230
or if it's outside your
normal working hours,

00:25:55.230 --> 00:25:58.050
then the threshold-- the bar--
of actually buzzing your phone

00:25:58.050 --> 00:26:00.390
is going to be higher
than if you're at work

00:26:00.390 --> 00:26:04.180
or not doing anything
in particular.

00:26:04.180 --> 00:26:05.470
Changing type slightly.

00:26:05.470 --> 00:26:08.080
So we talked about
snooze, and how

00:26:08.080 --> 00:26:11.020
snooze could help you make sure
that you didn't drop the ball

00:26:11.020 --> 00:26:12.602
and forget to reply.

00:26:12.602 --> 00:26:13.810
But snooze is kind of manual.

00:26:13.810 --> 00:26:15.482
You still have to
actually decide

00:26:15.482 --> 00:26:17.190
when you're going to
snooze that email to

00:26:17.190 --> 00:26:20.260
and that you want to
snooze that email.

00:26:20.260 --> 00:26:22.630
So in this
exploration, again, we

00:26:22.630 --> 00:26:26.650
use NLP to automatically detect
which emails are requesting

00:26:26.650 --> 00:26:29.760
a response from you, or why
you have requested a response

00:26:29.760 --> 00:26:31.960
in an email you've sent.

00:26:31.960 --> 00:26:34.510
And if there's been no
response in either direction

00:26:34.510 --> 00:26:37.030
for a certain period
of time, we'll

00:26:37.030 --> 00:26:38.930
bump those to the
top of your inbox.

00:26:38.930 --> 00:26:42.580
Again telling you, hey, you
haven't replied for three days,

00:26:42.580 --> 00:26:44.260
or perhaps, you want
to follow this up

00:26:44.260 --> 00:26:45.885
because the other
person hasn't replied

00:26:45.885 --> 00:26:48.230
for some amount of time.

00:26:48.230 --> 00:26:51.350
OK, so let's go
back to Calendar.

00:26:51.350 --> 00:26:53.960
So we talked a
lot about the fact

00:26:53.960 --> 00:26:55.640
that we're bad at saying no.

00:26:55.640 --> 00:26:59.744
So what if Calendar said no
so that you didn't have to?

00:26:59.744 --> 00:27:01.160
So in this
exploration, we've been

00:27:01.160 --> 00:27:04.280
thinking about, what
if we let users specify

00:27:04.280 --> 00:27:05.870
a limit on how
much time they want

00:27:05.870 --> 00:27:10.100
to spend on meetings each week?

00:27:10.100 --> 00:27:12.350
15 hours in this case.

00:27:12.350 --> 00:27:14.570
Essentially, taking that
pie chart of time-use

00:27:14.570 --> 00:27:17.630
that Jacob was talking about
and making it an integral part

00:27:17.630 --> 00:27:18.660
of the product.

00:27:18.660 --> 00:27:19.710
So what could we do?

00:27:19.710 --> 00:27:23.660
Well essentially, we could
change the scheduling

00:27:23.660 --> 00:27:24.330
environment.

00:27:24.330 --> 00:27:26.500
Not for you, but for
all of your colleagues

00:27:26.500 --> 00:27:28.049
who try to schedule
time with you.

00:27:28.049 --> 00:27:30.340
So if they try and set up a
meeting with you that would

00:27:30.340 --> 00:27:33.250
violate your limits,
we can push back--

00:27:33.250 --> 00:27:35.440
before that invite
has gone out--

00:27:35.440 --> 00:27:37.470
and we can actually
tell them, hey, there's

00:27:37.470 --> 00:27:39.340
a cost of scheduling
with you, right?

00:27:39.340 --> 00:27:40.840
Because you're busy.

00:27:40.840 --> 00:27:43.720
And we can provide a
low-friction way out

00:27:43.720 --> 00:27:44.830
by suggesting times.

00:27:44.830 --> 00:27:47.440
You can see that here
in the middle screenshot

00:27:47.440 --> 00:27:48.715
where you're both available.

00:27:48.715 --> 00:27:49.840
Where you've both got time.

00:27:49.840 --> 00:27:52.250
Where you're not
violating the limits.

00:27:52.250 --> 00:27:55.480
And if they really want to have
that meeting with you anyway

00:27:55.480 --> 00:27:58.840
because maybe it is
urgent, we nudge them

00:27:58.840 --> 00:28:01.780
into actually justifying
themselves to you

00:28:01.780 --> 00:28:04.450
rather than just sending
an anonymous invite

00:28:04.450 --> 00:28:06.530
and leaving all
work of saying no

00:28:06.530 --> 00:28:09.452
and justifying not going to you.

00:28:09.452 --> 00:28:15.170
OK, finally, we thought
about what would Goals

00:28:15.170 --> 00:28:18.770
look like if it was
focused on making sure

00:28:18.770 --> 00:28:21.320
that you were allocating
enough focused time

00:28:21.320 --> 00:28:22.940
to do productive work?

00:28:22.940 --> 00:28:26.330
And so we thought about a couple
of things in this exploration.

00:28:26.330 --> 00:28:28.610
Obviously, we've talked
a lot about making sure

00:28:28.610 --> 00:28:30.560
that time is scheduled
in the morning,

00:28:30.560 --> 00:28:32.685
and making sure we get
other things out of the way.

00:28:32.685 --> 00:28:35.120
So you can have your
focused time in the morning.

00:28:35.120 --> 00:28:37.100
We looked at having a
do-not-disturb mode that

00:28:37.100 --> 00:28:40.340
could be even more aggressive
at getting rid of disruptions,

00:28:40.340 --> 00:28:43.730
like for example, stopping
Hangout's chat notifications

00:28:43.730 --> 00:28:46.160
from happening.

00:28:46.160 --> 00:28:49.220
In addition, when we talk to
people who currently block out

00:28:49.220 --> 00:28:51.710
time manually on Calendar
for "make time today"

00:28:51.710 --> 00:28:54.590
or use the Goals feature,
a common complaint

00:28:54.590 --> 00:28:56.810
was that their colleagues
ignore the block.

00:28:56.810 --> 00:28:59.120
They say, oh, it's a work block.

00:28:59.120 --> 00:29:01.790
I can schedule over that because
they're not in a meeting.

00:29:01.790 --> 00:29:05.090
So again, we want to change
the scheduling environment.

00:29:05.090 --> 00:29:07.160
So we're calling out
the fact that if you're

00:29:07.160 --> 00:29:10.010
going to interrupt someone, that
there's a cost to doing that,

00:29:10.010 --> 00:29:11.593
and here are some
times which are more

00:29:11.593 --> 00:29:15.110
appropriate for having a meeting
rather than doing focused work.

00:29:15.110 --> 00:29:17.810
OK, so that's some
of the ways we're

00:29:17.810 --> 00:29:22.130
thinking about changing Gmail
and Calendar in the future.

00:29:22.130 --> 00:29:25.820
I hope this has been interesting
and a good use of your time.

00:29:25.820 --> 00:29:29.150
In conclusion, we
think that when

00:29:29.150 --> 00:29:32.190
it comes to managing your time
better, the environment is key.

00:29:32.190 --> 00:29:34.800
So if you only remember
three things from today,

00:29:34.800 --> 00:29:36.350
this is what we'd recommend.

00:29:36.350 --> 00:29:39.290
First of all, use the Google
Mail and Calendar clients

00:29:39.290 --> 00:29:43.780
on your phone and tablet
as well as on the laptop.

00:29:43.780 --> 00:29:46.280
It will help you today, and
it's going to help you even more

00:29:46.280 --> 00:29:47.550
in the future.

00:29:47.550 --> 00:29:51.260
Secondly, unless your role
at work is entirely reactive,

00:29:51.260 --> 00:29:53.210
block out a healthy
dose of "make time"

00:29:53.210 --> 00:29:55.250
in the calendar in the
mornings so that you

00:29:55.250 --> 00:29:57.050
can get productive work done.

00:29:57.050 --> 00:29:59.540
And tell your colleagues,
because if you all

00:29:59.540 --> 00:30:00.950
respect each
other's "make time,"

00:30:00.950 --> 00:30:04.310
you're all going to be more
effective and more productive.

00:30:04.310 --> 00:30:07.694
So with that, good luck
with your time management.

00:30:07.694 --> 00:30:09.110
Thank you very
much for listening.

00:30:09.110 --> 00:30:10.310
[APPLAUSE]

00:30:10.310 --> 00:30:13.360
[MUSIC PLAYING]

